  "solution": {
    "text": "For a substance to be called amphoteric, it should be able to react as a base as well as an acid. Hence, when it is reacted with a base, it should act as an acid, and when it is reacted with an acid, it should behave as a base.<br /><br />The reactions for amphoteric nature of Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>. x H<sub>2</sub>O(s) are<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>Al</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <msub> <mtext>O</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mo>.</mo> <mi>x</mi> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mtext>O</mtext> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>s</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> <mover> <mo>&#8594;</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>OH</mtext> <mtext>aq</mtext> <mo>-</mo> </msubsup> </mover> <mtext>Al</mtext> <msubsup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>OH</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>4</mn> <mo>-</mo> </msubsup> <mo>aq</mo> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;<br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;Aluminate anion<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>Al</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <msub> <mtext>O</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mo>.</mo> <mi>x</mi> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mtext>O</mtext> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>s</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> <mover> <mo>&#8594;</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>H</mtext> <mtext>aq</mtext> <mo>+</mo> </msubsup> </mover> <msubsup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>Al</mtext> <msub> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mtext>O</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>6</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mtext>aq</mtext> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mo>+</mo> </mrow> </msubsup> </mrow> </math>.<br /><br />[Al(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> is octahedral complex ion<br /><br />[Al(OH)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>-1</sup> tetrahedral at pH = 13"